 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Spatial Relationship Artifacts:** In Maximum Importance Projection (MImP), using a cylindrical projection geometry can cause visual artifacts where the spatial relationships between focus and context objects are not clearly shown, specifically making the focus object appear in front of the context object incorrectly [S2, unofficial]. This can be corrected by using a conical projection geometry with different slope factors to define the countersink geometry [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Occlusion Handling:** Standard DVR combines color/opacity values regardless of importance, meaning high-importance hidden objects cannot be visualized entirely [S3, unofficial]. IDVR ensures critical data is represented correctly despite occlusion [S3, unofficial].
+*   **MImP Artifacts:** Using a cylindrical projection geometry in Maximum Importance Projection (MImP) can cause visual artifacts where spatial relationships between focus and context objects are not clearly shown, specifically making the focus object appear in front of the context object incorrectly [S2, unofficial]. This is corrected by using a conical projection geometry with different slope factors to define the countersink geometry [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Occlusion Evaluation:** Determining which object occludes which requires accurate depth information and footprint calculation. Incorrect footprint calculation leads to incorrect sparseness assignment [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Screen-Door Transparency with Importance Compositing](8197-screen-door-transparency-with-importance-compositing.md)
+Connects feature importance to spatial sampling density using screen-door transparency. It solves the problem of occluding important features by less important ones by dynamically adjusting sampling density and opacity based on feature importance and occlusion relationships, rather than using a fixed global transfer function. Unlike Maximum Importance Projection (MImP), which renders only the feature with the highest importance along the ray, this method composites multiple features to preserve context while suppressing occluding less important structures.
+
 ### [Maximum Importance Projection (MImP)](8308-maximum-importance-projection-mimp.md)
-An importance-based compositing method that clips unimportant parts of the volume data based on importance values. It renders only the feature with the highest importance along the ray. It is chosen when complete clipping of unimportant object parts is desired. A pitfall is the spatial relationship artifact with cylindrical projection geometry, which can be mitigated by conical projection.
-
-### Screen-Door Transparency with Importance Compositing
-A rendering technique that connects feature importance to spatial sampling density using screen-door transparency [S1, unofficial]. Focus features are rendered densely, while context features are rendered sparsely if they occlude focus features, or densely otherwise [S1, unofficial]. It differs from traditional viewpoint-independent classification by dynamically adjusting sampling density and opacity based on feature importance and occlusion relationships, rather than using a fixed global transfer function [S1, unofficial].
+An importance-based compositing method that clips unimportant parts of volume data based on assigned importance values. It assigns the lowest sparseness value (densest representation) to the object with the maximum importance along a ray, while all other objects receive the highest sparseness value, effectively rendering them either densely or not at all. Unlike more complex importance-based methods that allow reduced opacity representations for less important objects, MImP is simpler to implement but offers less control over sparseness selection.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds an additional step (importance compositing) to the standard rendering pipeline, which traverses the volume to identify object occlusions [S1, unofficial]. This increases computational cost compared to standard DVR.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing importance values for each voxel, adding a third dimension to opacity and color [S3, unofficial]. Also requires footprint data for each volume object [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Automatically removes unwanted occlusions and maximizes the information content of the final image [S2, unofficial]. Allows users to view hidden but important objects regardless of view direction [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires predefined importance values for each voxel [S3, unofficial]. May introduce visual artifacts if not implemented correctly (e.g., cylindrical MImP) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When the most interesting features are occluded by less important features and traditional viewpoint-independent approaches are insufficient [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When importance values are not available or not meaningful for the dataset.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The addition of the Importance Compositing step increases rendering time. It requires traversing the volume to evaluate occlusion and assign sparseness levels, which is an additional pass or integrated logic within the ray-casting loop [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing importance values for each voxel or cluster, and potentially footprint data for each object [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Automatically removes unwanted occlusions and maximizes information content. Allows users to view hidden but important objects regardless of view direction. Provides a generalization of cut-away views [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-defined importance values, which may need user interaction or complex automatic clustering [S4]. MImP can introduce spatial relationship artifacts if not implemented with conical projection [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When visualizing complex volumetric data where certain structures are critical but often occluded by less important surrounding tissue (e.g., medical imaging, scientific visualization) [S2, unofficial][S4].
+*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance is critical and the overhead of importance evaluation is unacceptable, or when all structures are of equal importance [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Importance-Driven Volume Rendering was introduced by Ivan Viola, Armin Kanitsar, and Eduard Gröller in 2004 [S2, unofficial]. It is an addition to the common rendering pipeline and can be efficiently combined with other known volume visualization methods, such as LMIP, Contour, Tone, or Direct volume rendering using Phong illumination model [S3, unofficial]. The technique is a generalization of cut-away views, which remove or suppress less important parts of a scene to reveal more important underlying information [S2, unofficial].
+Importance-Driven Volume Rendering was introduced by Ivan Viola, Armin Kanitsar, and Eduard Gröller in 2004 at the 15th IEEE Visualization Conference (VIS 2004) [S2, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of traditional Direct Volume Rendering (DVR), where high-importance hidden objects cannot be visualized entirely due to occlusion by less important objects [S3, unofficial]. The technique extends the raycasting pipeline by integrating importance compositing, which evaluates occlusion and assigns levels of sparseness [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It is often embedded into DVR and combined with standard rendering methods like LMIP, Contour, Tone, or Direct volume rendering using Phong illumination model [S3, unofficial].
 
